this is a production of Cornell  University  great thanks for that introduction Jacob  I didn't realize today was cassava  themed but at least I'll be talking  about a rabbi list which was a daikon so  there we go so for today I just want to  generally introduce the concepts behind  one of my PhD projects to use deep  learning models to model transcriptional  processes across rabid OPS's and maize  and also show a little bit of the  preliminary data that I've done from  this work so just to start off we're all  very aware of the different  methodologies used to locate causal si  over the past decades of breeding and  we're familiar with key TL mapping and  also G wasps that we've recently  switched to because of its higher  resolution but we're still limited in  our resolution because of linkage and  it's still very difficult to separate  correlated variance with the actual  causal variance and so the question  always remains how can we increase the  resolution to target those actual causal  barriers  another thing we need to consider is  that at least with QTL and gos you are  directly relating the phenotype with the  genotype data through a linear model and  you're generally ignoring the complex  nonlinear biological processes that  underline these and at least recent  studies have have begun to associates or  the quantitative difference in genotype  with quantitative differences at the RNA  level the protein level and also  metabolite level and today I'll be  focusing on how I'm looking at the  transcriptional level between DNA and  RNA so the flavor of deep learning model  that I'm using is called a convolutional  neural network and you might be familiar  with the term from the context of image  recognition or image classification  where it's the current state of the art  and since convolution is a matrix  operation the first thing we're going to  do so our DNA is converted into a matrix  and so the way we do that is we're  simply going to have a matrix the length  of our sequence and for columns for our  ACTG and we're just going to put ones in  each column corresponding to the bases  so we're going to end up with a sparse  matrix of zeros and ones then  we're gonna do is we're going to take  our orange box here which is basically  our sliding window we also have  corresponding matrices known as filters  which I like to think of as sort of like  position weight matrices for those  familiar with that basically it's a  matrix that weights base occurrence in  certain positions of the window over  others what you're going to do is you're  going to multiply those two matrices  well do a convolution on two of those  matrices and the result you expect to be  higher in the right column which would  be a very dark red color if the two  matrices are more similar so if your  motif is in the window and so the way we  can sort of visualize that is if we  slide along the sequence and now we've  hit a match and we get a high output  value in the right-hand column so the  next thing we're going to do is we're  going to apply what's known as an  activation function and in this case  it's what's known as a real OOP  all the real ooh does is turn all the  negative values to zero and keeps all  your positive values the same way and  this is this is one of the examples of  nonlinear functions that we can use in  in neural networks so one diagram you  may be familiar with if you've seen a  couple of deep learning slides before is  this architecture diagram of the fully  connected layer these are typically done  after your convolutional layers and they  basically consists of a bunch of neurons  that are connected to each these are the  white dots to each of the output labels  from the previous layer with a series of  edges that each have their associated  weights and so all you're going to do  for each of those neurons is take each  of your output data multiply it by the  respective weight sum that result and  tie another activation function that's  your result and you're going to do this  for each layer of your model and then  your final layers your output layer and  that's just going to have what value you  want which in this in my case would be  mRNA expression levels so all of that is  assuming you have all the parameters  that you want you have your filter  matrices estimated you have those  weights s and but what if you're  starting from scratch with a data set  say RNA seek and you want to estimate  those parameters how do you do that  and so we just call it training neural  networks or training models which is  essentially  getting a model to the data and the way  we do that is we divide our input data  up into batches for each those batches  well first we're gonna initialize all  the parameters to small random values  and then we're going to take our first  batch we're gonna make a prediction  which should be pretty bad because it's  just a random model at this point then  we're gonna take the air from that  prediction and the model architecture  and feed that into a training algorithm  and that training algorithm is going to  tell us the direction to update those  parameters to minimize our air we're  going to repeat those steps until we're  out of data that's one epoch and then  normally you would run a couple of  epochs to converge on an accurate model  I do want to emphasize that these  filters I showed earlier they start out  random because you initialize them  randomly and as a consequence of the  training process they gradually become  more defined as motifs if you think of  them as pwms this way matrices and that  is one way that these CN NS can extract  features from your data because you  don't give it these filters in advance  it learns how to accurately predict  expression by learning these motifs so  one of the common criticisms of deep  learning models is that they're even  though they're highly predictive and  they get strong accuracies how do we  interpret what they're actually learning  and they're generally considered to have  low interpret ability but in recent  years there's been a number of  techniques to sort of open up that black  box and interpret what the model is  actually learning one of the example of  this is deep lift and the way deep lift  works simply is by taking a random  background sequence or a reference  sequence it could be some random DNA or  maybe tune to the background frequencies  of your specific organism and then you  take a true positive let's say a gene  that you expect to have high expression  you run those through the model and then  you can directly compare the  intermediate layers of the model and  determine basically what parts of the  input are most predictive of changes in  the output so some  results from or not recent but some  previous results from a postdoc in our  lab hi wang who gave the deep learning  seminar at the beginning of the semester  he took three ideas one was to use the  promoter sequence defined as 15 but  hundred base pairs around the  transcription start site the terminator  sequence is just 1500 based prisoner and  the transcription termination site and  also a combination of the two to predict  expression using a CNN and his results  are on the right and you can basically  see that the promoter and the Terminator  model did the best with our Squared's of  about 0.45 and I do want to emphasize  that though those R Squared's aren't  that high we don't expect to have an R  squared of 1 to predict expression from  sis regulatory sequence because that we  know that biologically that doesn't  explain all the variation there so these  are actually pretty good considering the  input data so another thing  talk about is how we can possibly extend  this mRNA model to generalize different  tissues so we know that the DNA is the  same in different tissues but things  like epigenetics can change but the  model isn't currently taking that into  account and so one way we can think of  is by adding extra columns to this  matrix that correspond to epigenetic  signals that would be present and differ  between tissues so that the model can  learn how to predict differences in  tissue expression and so one example to  be methylation open chromatin signals  histone modifications and also other  epigenetic marks and another concept  touch upon is transfer learning and the  way I like to explain it is teaching an  old dog new tricks you have a model that  you've already trained on one large data  set that achieves high accuracy and now  you want to apply that model in another  related data set that's probably smaller  so if you train a model from scratch you  would not get as high of an accuracy  probably but what you can do is freeze  some of the parameters in the first  model that's been trained on the related  data set and retrain only part of the  model in the smaller data set and you  would expect to achieve slightly higher  accuracy as the smaller data so how does  that apply in our case well if you take  a an organism like rabid offices which  generally has a more diverse and larger  collection of RNA seek data sets you  might say that we could train more  accurate models to predict a rabid  Office's expression and so because we  have a lot more data there but if we  want to transfer into a crop species  that probably doesn't have the diverse  array of data as a rabid abscess well  how could we do that well so if we go  back and think about the convolutional  neural network that I introduced earlier  this part of the network it's sort of  how you learn to it learns to identify  motifs at least of the convolution stage  and then the neural network stage is  really where it learns how these motifs  interact together to regulate and  modulate expression and so if we in this  example make the assumption that between  let's say a rabid office in maze these  motifs are conserved that drive  expression let's say their transcription  factor binding sites that don't change  but the way they relate to each other  and drive high or low expression differs  so what we can do is take an arab adaxes  train model erase let's say the last  layers weights and then retrain it on  May's data just those last parameters  and now we have a model that doesn't  have to relearn how to recognize motifs  but can just simply be tuned to the  species specific connections  and so this is some preliminary results  that I've done in a rabid Oxus using hi  small and so what you're looking at on  the left is a prediction of mean  expression across all tissues for all  genes and rabbit houses and what you can  sort of tell is that it's similar in  accuracy to highs model and if you  compare generally the distributions  across all tissues is the models what I  want to emphasize the models have  comparable results so they do also work  in a rabbit Alice's okay so the  questions that I want to answer with the  rest of this work is basically can these  multi tissue transcriptional models be  trained with epigenetic information is  this a good idea and will it generalize  across tissues another question I'd like  to add answer is can we train models on  one species for example a rabbit OPS's  and then use those as useful starting  models to retrain and retune in other  species to achieve similar accuracies  but with less available data all right  and with  just like to acknowledge the buckler lab  and specifically Heidi and Katherine and  Emery for their discussions on this  project as well as my funding sources  and I'd be happy to take any questions  [Applause]  great questions so the question was am i  training on only one or Abydos this  genotype or all that potentially  thousand and one so currently I'm only  training on Colombia zero the reference  genotype but the plan is to use all  thousand and one genotypes are there any  questions from zoom don't think so  okay well thank you very much Janice of  Cornell University on the web at Cornell  edu

